<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirza Hasan Shirazi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1815–1895</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirza Hasan Shirazi was an Iranian Shi'i jurist, the leading religious authority of his time, who taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> in Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. In December 1891 a ruling attributed to him declared the use of tobacco forbidden while a British company held the Iranian tobacco monopoly, and the resulting nationwide boycott, the Tobacco Protest, forced Naser al-Din Shah to cancel the concession in 1892.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirza-hasan-shirazi.jpg" alt="Mirza Hasan Shirazi" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirza_Sayyid_Mohammed_Hassan_Al-Husseini_Al-Shirazi_praying.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
